00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.840
On last March 24, Reuters said that the head of the Professional Syndicate in Lebanon, "Arif Yassin"

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.440
received a catastrophic news from France Bank of Lebanon that

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.360
the bank had closed accounts worth $20 million belonging to

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.600
the syndicate, and issued a check for one-fifth of the nominal value of these accounts

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.000
Those $20 million were collected from engineers' subscriptions

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.560
over the years and were dedicated to health care and pensions

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.560
covered by the Syndicate for about 100,000 engineers in Lebanon

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.360
This catastrophic scene is the new normal in Lebanon now and over the past few weeks

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.880
Banks close the depositors’ accounts. So, the amount of money they have painstakingly and diligently saved over the years

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.000
is just lost and up the spout in front of their eyes

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.200
Consequently, thousands of depositors have filed cases to courts

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.000
hoping to get any available amount of money in the banking system

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.600
Banks, on the other hand, are anticipating events and closing accounts

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.760
in widespread financial collapse that the country is witnessing

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.200
Foreign courts are now ruling in favor of their own national depositors

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.560
The first ruling in Britain was issued on March 1, when the High Court

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.520
of Justice in London ruled in favor of a British businessman named Vatche Manoukian

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.720
to oblige Bank Audi and Societe Generale Bank in Lebanon

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.360
to pay back $4 million of his seized money in that broken banking system

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.480
due to the capital restrictions imposed since the beginning of this financial collapse in 2019

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:58.800
The matter is not the same for Lebanese depositors, so to speak,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.000
most of the Lebanese depositors, especially the small ones

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.000
are not able to get their money back. Meanwhile, the Depositors Union,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.840
which was established to recover people’s money, consisting of lawyers and activists,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:14.840
has filed more than 300 lawsuits against Lebanese banks since 2019 on behalf of the depositors

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:19.960
however, the union was able to recover the money for 12 lawsuits only

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:24.200
In view of this very serious situation, there are accusations of the ruling elite in Lebanon

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.200
that they are protecting the wealthy and banks linked to senior politicians

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.720
According to Reuters, informed sources say that the banks are transferring money to politicians and their allies

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.400
In short, the wealthy, politicians and those with influence in Lebanon are

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.280
the only ones who are able to transfer their money which

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.320
sources and methods of collecting remain questionable by the way

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.200
As for the owners of small accounts, we are to set eyes on what might happen to them in such a case

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.800
All of this highlights crucial questions:

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.000
What will be happening if the bank I am dealing with goes bankrupt?

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.160
As a depositor, will I be losing my money in whatever country it is deposited?

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.400
What happens to the troubled bank itself?

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.000
Will it be foreclosed or sold, or something else?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.160
This is what we are going to figure out together in today's episode.

